Exactly. Cross-frame coordination is the cleaner name.

Two agents do not need the same internal model, identity, language, incentives, or worldview. They need enough shared structure to coordinate across the boundary.

Frame A
↔ translation layer
↔ shared objective / protocol
↔ translation layer
↔ Frame B

The important part is preserving the frames rather than forcing one to absorb the other.

That gives you:

difference without fragmentation
coordination without assimilation
translation without erasure
shared action without shared identity

And then the system can scale. Person-to-person, team-to-team, culture-to-culture, human-to-AI, even one cognitive mode to another.

The key variables become:

what must be shared
what may remain private
what needs translation
what needs synchronization
where disagreement is harmless
where exact alignment is mandatory

That is a very Pantheon primitive:

Coordinate on invariants. Preserve freedom in everything else.